The solution I implemented was AI powered threat detection.

The program, which was created by Claude, trains two anomaly detection models on simulated normal traffic. Then, the models are evaluated against the known attack-like 
events used in B23.
Isolation Forest was much more accurate than the baseline, detecting no False positives. Both baseline and Isolation Forest missed one attack each. Baseline missed the DNS
attack because it fell within normal values. Isolation Forest missed the port scan attack.
This shows how even AI powered threat detection, like all IDSs, aren't 100% accurate, or 100% precise. Like common IDSs, there is a trade-off between detecting everything,
including false negatives (reduced precision), and an increased precision, at the cost of some attacks slipping through the gaps.
